# Direction B (plan v2.9 Sec. 3 B): stage-wise dynamics under weight-decay schedules — working note

Owner: `experiment-repo`. Status: development (2026-10-18). Code: `code/e5_theory.py` (stage model, baselines),
`code/e5_b1.py` (B1 grids and scoring). Tolerances: `results/e5/b1_tolerance.json`, committed before any B1 grid ran.
Labels as in `theory/derivations.md`: **exact** (proved, checked), **approximation** (derived with a stated
approximation; accuracy measured), **hypothesis** (to be tested).

## Setting
The delivered linear JEPA (derivations Sec. 1) with aligned statistics and an aligned initialisation (Prop. 3), so
each direction k is the scalar system
$$\dot w=p\,c\,\bar w-(p^2\sigma+\lambda_W(t))\,w,\qquad \dot p=\alpha\,[(c\bar w-p\sigma w)w-\lambda_P(t)\,p],$$
with $\bar w=w$ (copy target) or $\dot{\bar w}=\kappa(w-\bar w)$. Weight decay is now a function of $t=\eta\cdot$step;
$\Lambda_P(t)=\int_0^t\lambda_P$. The static threshold of direction k is $\lambda_k^*=c_k^2/(4\sigma_k)$ (Prop. 4).

## B-0. The balance identity holds for any schedule (exact)
For $\alpha=1$, a copy target and $\lambda_W(t)=\lambda_P(t)=\lambda(t)$,
$$p^2-w^2=e^{-2\Lambda(t)}\,(p_0^2-w_0^2).$$
*Proof.* $\tfrac{d}{dt}p^2=2[(c-p\sigma)w^2p-\lambda p^2]$ and $\tfrac{d}{dt}w^2=2[(pc-p^2\sigma)w^2-\lambda w^2]$, so
$\tfrac{d}{dt}(p^2-w^2)=-2\lambda(t)(p^2-w^2)$. $\square$ So Cor. 3a holds with $\lambda t\to\Lambda(t)$: the direction is
an exact one-dimensional non-autonomous system. With $\lambda_P\ne\lambda_W$ the difference obeys
$\tfrac{d}{dt}(p^2-w^2)=-2\lambda_Pp^2+2\lambda_Ww^2$ and no identity holds. Prop. 3 itself (the matrix flow is n
independent scalar systems) does not use constant hyperparameters, so it holds for every schedule (checked as B1-0).

## B-i. Race (approximation A1 with a schedule)
While the direction is small, the predictor decays under its own weight decay only, $p_r(t)=p_0e^{-\alpha\Lambda_P(t)}$,
and $\log w(t)=\log w_0+\int_0^t g(p_r(\tau),\lambda_W(\tau))\,d\tau$ with the delivered growth rate $g$ (the EMA root
when $\kappa$ is finite). A schedule changes the race in two ways: the window $(p_-,p_+)$ of $g$ moves with
$\lambda_W(t)$, and the predictor's clock is $\Lambda_P(t)$ rather than $\lambda t$.

## B-ii. Capture (approximation)
The direction captures the predictor at the first $t$ with $g>0$ and $w^2\ge\lambda_P(t)\,p_r/(c-p_r\sigma)$, the
delivered nullcline evaluated at the current $\lambda_P$.

## B-iii. Post-capture: the slaved predictor and the moving static threshold (approximation)
At the stable fixed point, $\dot p=0$ gives $w^2(c-p\sigma)=\lambda_Pp$ and $\dot w=0$ gives $pc-p^2\sigma=\lambda_W$,
so $p=w\sqrt{\lambda_W/\lambda_P}$ (balanced, $p=w$, when $\lambda_W=\lambda_P$, as B-0 also gives once
$e^{-2\Lambda}$ is small). After capture the stage model keeps the predictor on this relation,
$$\dot w=(pc-p^2\sigma-\lambda_W(t))\,w,\qquad p=w\sqrt{\lambda_W(t)/\lambda_P(t)},$$
started at the stable fixed point $p_+(\lambda_W(t_c))$. Consequences:
- **Loss at the crossing.** A captured direction survives while $\lambda_W(t)\le\lambda_k^*$: the fixed point
  disappears in a saddle-node when $\lambda_W$ passes $\lambda_k^*$. For a step it is lost after a bottleneck delay;
  for a ramp, after the crossing time $\lambda_W(t)=\lambda_k^*$ plus the delay. A step that stays below
  $\lambda_k^*$ is survived, because $p_+(\lambda_1)>c/(2\sigma)>p_-(\lambda_2)$ for any two admissible values.
- **No regrowth below the separatrix; the post-loss predictor (revised twice).** After a loss the window closes as
  $w$ decays: at small $w$, $g\approx-\lambda_W$, so $w\propto e^{-\Lambda_W(t)}$. The $p$ equation is linear in $p$
  once $p\sigma w^2$ is negligible,
  $$\dot p=-\alpha\lambda_P\,p+\alpha c\,\bar w w,\qquad p(t)=e^{-\alpha\Lambda_P(t)}\Big[p_\ell e^{\alpha\Lambda_P(t_\ell)}+\alpha c\int_{t_\ell}^{t}\bar w w\,e^{\alpha\Lambda_P}\,d\tau\Big],$$
  so $p$ decays at the slower of its own rate $\alpha\lambda_P$ and the rate of its drive $\bar ww$ ($2\lambda_W$ for a
  copy target; $\lambda_W+\min(\lambda_W,\kappa)$ for an EMA target) (**approximation**). On every grid here
  $\lambda_P=\rho\lambda_W(t)$, so in the clock $\Lambda_W$: $-d\ln w/d\Lambda_W\to1$, $-d\ln p/d\Lambda_W\to\min(\alpha\rho,2)$,
  and $\ln(p/w)$ moves with slope $1-\min(\alpha\rho,2)$. The sign rule is therefore $\mathrm{sign}(\lambda_W-\alpha\lambda_P)$:
  - $\alpha\lambda_P<\lambda_W$ ($\alpha<1$ or $\rho<1$): $p$ is on its own decay and rises above the slaved value
    $w\sqrt{\lambda_W/\lambda_P}$ without bound;
  - $\alpha\lambda_P=\lambda_W$: $p/w$ is held at its value at the loss. For $\alpha=1$, $\lambda_P=\lambda_W$ that value
    is the slaved one, $p/w=1$ (B-0 for a copy target; measured for the EMA target too), so **the slaved relation holds
    after a loss in this case only**;
  - $\alpha\lambda_P>\lambda_W$: $p$ falls faster than $w$, below the slaved value without bound, but no faster than
    $\min(\alpha\lambda_P,2\lambda_W)$. For $\alpha\lambda_P>2\lambda_W$ it rides the forced solution
    $p\approx\alpha c\,w^2/(\alpha\lambda_P-2\lambda_W)$ (constant $\lambda$), above its own decay. At the resonance
    $\alpha\lambda_P=2\lambda_W$ (the development cells $\alpha=2$ and $\rho_P=2$), $p\,e^{2\Lambda_W}=C+\alpha c\,w_\ell^2\int d\Lambda_W/\lambda_W$:
    $p$ sits above its own decay by the log of a growing factor, its rate approaches $2\lambda_W$ from below, and the
    deficit returns when $\lambda_W$ falls (the forcing per unit $\Lambda_W$ grows as $1/\lambda_W$).

  Measured on the development grid (`code/checks/e5_postloss_law.py` -> `results/e5/b1dev/posthoc/postloss_law.json`,
  consecutive dead checkpoints of truly lost directions, first dead interval excluded): the median of
  $-d\ln w/d\Lambda_W$ is 1.000 in every variant, and of $-d\ln p/d\Lambda_W$ 0.500 for $\alpha$ 0.5 and $\rho_P$ 0.5
  (predicted 0.5; $p/w$ rising on 99.6-99.7% of intervals), 1.000 for base and ema0.1 (predicted 1), and 1.87/1.88 for $\alpha$ 2 /
  $\rho_P$ 2 (predicted 2 at resonance, approached from below: 1.74-1.75 at $\Delta\Lambda_W\approx5$, 1.97-1.99 by
  $\Delta\Lambda_W\approx25$-$45$, back to 1.72-1.76 after the high pulse ends; $p/w$ falling on 99%). The split of
  $\ln(p/(w\sqrt{\lambda_W/\lambda_P}))$ on the truth's own $w$ (`results/e5/b1dev/posthoc/sched_v3.json` `by_variant`,
  pooled post-loss pairs of the 313 schedule runs; over all 320 runs with a loss, constant $\lambda$ included, the
  internal review's check gives +13.7/+13.9 for $\alpha$ 0.5/$\rho_P$ 0.5 and the same values in the other four) has
  the predicted signs: median 0.00
  for base and ema0.1, +14.1/+14.0 for $\alpha$ 0.5/$\rho_P$ 0.5, -18.1/-15.2 for $\alpha$ 2/$\rho_P$ 2, with the
  truth 3.8/3.4 nats above its own decay in the last two (the resonant log factor). Two further checks (same file): at the resonance
  $(2-\text{slope}_p)\,\Delta\Lambda_W$ divided by $\bar\lambda_W/\lambda_W(t)$ (mean $\lambda_W$ since the loss over the
  current one, the limit the law gives since $p\,e^{2\Delta\Lambda_W}$ grows linearly in $t$) has median 1.02/1.01
  for $\alpha$ 2/$\rho_P$ 2 (1.05/1.05 on the later half); and a level test, $p$ integrated from $(p,w)$ at the first
  dead checkpoint by $\dot p=-\alpha\lambda_P p+\alpha c w^2$ with the truth's $w$ (copy-target variants, the internal
  review's suggestion), misses the truth by a median $|\ln|$ of 0.071 (base), 0.027 ($\alpha$ 0.5), 0.097 ($\alpha$ 2),
  0.029 ($\rho_P$ 0.5) and 0.096 ($\rho_P$ 2), q90 at most 0.34, against 2.67/2.49 for own decay alone in $\alpha$ 2/$\rho_P$ 2
  (0.02-0.34 with $w=w_\ell e^{-\Delta\Lambda_W}$). So the forced term accounts for the excess over own decay in level,
  not only in rate. An earlier version of this note
  said that $p/w$ is never held and cited a 10-run sample ("truth $p$ far above"); both were wrong for the
  $\alpha=1$, $\lambda_P=\lambda_W$ cells and for $\alpha\lambda_P>\lambda_W$.

  A later decrease of $\lambda_W$ revives the direction if $p$ is then inside the reopened window and $w$ has not
  underflowed; the regrowth time is $\int dw/(w\,g)$. Against this law the delivered stage model (slaved $p$, rate
  $\lambda_W$) is right only for $\alpha\lambda_P=\lambda_W$, falls too fast for $\alpha\lambda_P<\lambda_W$ and too
  slowly for $\alpha\lambda_P>\lambda_W$. `stage_model_qs` (frozen for the held-out grid) takes the larger of its own
  decay and the static nullcline $c w^2/(\lambda_P+\sigma w^2)$: right for $\alpha\lambda_P<\lambda_W$, and for
  $\alpha\lambda_P\ge\lambda_W$ below the truth, because the static nullcline ignores that $w$ itself decays (the
  factor $\alpha\lambda_P/(\alpha\lambda_P-2\lambda_W)$, unbounded at resonance). The development grid had no regain, so
  the held-out grid includes short high pulses, $\rho<1$ and $\alpha<1$, where the models differ.
- **Late decreases do not reopen the race (conditional).** A direction never captured is revived by a later
  decrease only if its predictor is still inside the reopened window. In the race this means only while the race is
  still running. The internal review's check found the exception (dev run 2781, step_down 0.12 -> 0.004 at t = 100,
  rho 0.5): a direction near the capture nullcline is held on a sub-capture plateau (a ~ 0.06, p ~ 0.1, above the
  decayed p_r) and regains when the window reopens. The claim therefore holds only for directions whose predictor
  has decayed to the race value; the development revision `stage_model_qs` keeps the plateau (the predictor follows
  its own nullcline given w, and falls no faster than its own decay), and the held-out registration states which
  model is frozen.

## B2. Consequences for schedules (derived; tested on the held-out grid)
- **Increasing schedule:** directions are lost at their crossing times $\lambda_W(t)=\lambda_k^*$ (plus the delay);
  the final rank is at most the static count at the final $\lambda$.
- **Warm-up (low during the race, raised after capture):** final rank $=\#\{k$ captured under the schedule and
  $\lambda_k^*\ge\lambda_{hi}\}$. This approaches the static count at $\lambda_{hi}$ only if the low-$\lambda$ race
  has captured those directions before the switch. A low $\lambda$ also slows the race (the predictor's clock is
  $\Lambda_P$), so a switch before the low-$\lambda$ race completes can end **below** the constant-$\lambda_{hi}$
  race count. The plan's premise ("approaches the static count at the final lambda") is therefore conditional on
  the switch time, and B1/B2 measure which case holds.
- **Decreasing schedule:** late decreases revive nothing that was lost or never captured once the race is over
  (B-iii); early decreases act like a lower constant $\lambda$.
- **Separate predictor decay** ($\lambda_P=\rho\lambda_W$): the window and the loss threshold depend on $\lambda_W$
  only; $\lambda_P$ sets the race clock, the capture nullcline and the post-capture amplitude
  $w^*=p_+\sqrt{\rho}$. Cor. 4a ($\lambda_P=0$: every direction collapses) is the limit $\rho\to0$.

A post hoc consistency, not a test: E8's production grid found that raising weight decay late in the ViT still
collapses while lowering it late barely recovers (`results/e8/grid/summary.json`). B-iii's no-regrowth part matches
this for arm T only: in arm P the lost directions regrow after the switch, more slowly than the remaining lr would
allow (`results/e8/grid/posthoc/`, commit 70cdda4). Whether B-iii is the ViT's mechanism is not tested here.

## B3. SGD noise in the linear copy-target model: multiplicative, faster decay of collapsed directions (registered; `results/e5/prediction_b3.json`)
Setting: the aligned model above with minibatch SGD (batch $B$, fresh Gaussian samples, step $\eta$), $P_0=p_0I$.
Near collapse the update is $W\leftarrow W(I+\eta\hat M)$, $\hat M=p\hat S_{21}-p^2\hat S_{11}-\lambda I$, so the
singular values of $W$ grow at the Lyapunov exponents of a random matrix product.

*Range invariance (exact).* Every SGD step multiplies $W$ on the right and adds $-\eta\,\nabla_P$-driven terms whose
output lies in $\mathrm{range}(P^\top W)$; with $P=pI$ plus terms in $\mathrm{range}(W)$ the output range of $W$ is
invariant. A direction that has collapsed to exactly zero therefore cannot be reseeded by the noise: SGD noise is
multiplicative here, as prior work assumes (window shifted to $g_k-D_k/2$), not additive. In float64 the only additive
source is round-off, about $10^{-16}$ of the top singular value.

*Accounts.* det: $g_k=pc_k-p^2s_k-\lambda$. shift (prior work): $g_k-(\eta/2B)\,\mathbb E\xi_{kk}^2$, with
$\mathbb E\xi_{kk}^2=p^2s_k^2(1+\rho_k^2-4p\rho_k+2p^2)$. lyap: a second-order expansion of the ordered exponents
(inflow from weaker modes, outflow to stronger ones). **It fails a direct check**: exponents computed by QR over
40,000 steps at fixed $p$ (development, $p\in\{0.8,1.3\}$, $B\in\{4,16\}$) all lie below $g_k$. The deficit grows
for weaker modes (to $-0.48$ at $p=1.3$, $B=4$). lyap instead predicts positive shifts for the strong modes, and the
discrete-time term $\log(1+\eta g)/\eta-g$ explains only $-0.01$ to $-0.03$ of the deficit. lyap is kept only as a row.
mc replaces it: the exponents are computed numerically (QR) on a grid of $p$ with $P$ frozen at $pI$.

*Development* (`results/e5/b3_dev/`, 96 runs, exploratory):
- Re-escape: one event in 59 noisy runs.
- Collapsed directions fall *faster* with more noise. The median log10 of (largest dead singular value / top) at
  $\eta=0.04$ is $-15.4$ at $B=2$, $-14.6$ at $B=4$, $-11.2$ at $B=16$ and $-8.7$ in the population. A floor would give
  the reverse order, with the level rising with $\eta/B$.
- Final rank: noise changes it by about one direction. The sign follows $p_0$: $-1.07$ at $p_0=0.8$ and $+1.31$ at
  $p_0=1.3$, relative to the population run with the same seed.
- MAE on noisy runs: shift 0.98, lyap 0.93, mc 1.14, det 1.19, early 1.07, window 5.4, static 8.6.
- No drift account produces the rank *increase* at $p_0>\rho$. All of them lower the rates. A first-passage
  (diffusion) effect of the log-amplitude near the capture scale is the candidate. It is not derived here.
- Steps of $\eta=0.1$ with $B\le4$ diverge. The held-out grid uses $\eta\le0.05$.

Registered items S1–S4 (code/e5_b3.py docstring; read as amended in `prediction_b3.json` `amendment_1`, before
any held-out run). **Scope.** Range invariance excludes reseeding and an additive floor *by construction* in this
model, so neither is an empirical question here: every term acting on a dead output direction is proportional to its
own amplitude, and its log-amplitude has a drift and a diffusion but no source. S1 (share of noisy runs with a
re-escape $\le0.05$) is therefore a numerical consistency check of the invariance; a re-escape under the code's
definition (alive, below THR, above THR again) can only be a nonzero singular value crossing THR back and forth, and a
failed S1 would mean such crossings, not reseeding. A separate exact check counts indices that reach round-off
($\le10^{-13}$ of the top) and later exceed THR (L3's round-off reseeding). S2 (dead level
$B_{small}<B_{large}<$ population) tests the shift account's prediction that noise *speeds* the decay of collapsed
directions ($-D_k/2$), against det (no $B$ dependence); levels at or below round-off are tied and the order is then
read on the dead log-slope. S3 bounds the rank effect; S4 is a measured regularity. The noise clause of N3 holds for
models with this invariance (linear, copy target, multiplicative minibatch noise). A lagging EMA target, additive
noise (augmentation, label noise) and nonlinear layers are outside it.

## Scope and what is not claimed
Aligned statistics and initialisation, full-batch GD. Generic initialisations are handled by N1-general (derivations
Sec. 6), noise by B3 (above). The slaved relation is exact only at the fixed point and for $\alpha=1$,
$\lambda_W=\lambda_P$ (B-0); for $\alpha\ne1$, EMA and $\rho\ne1$ it is an approximation measured in B1.
